# SOUNDNESS (P0): the completion proof's call-specific walk reports a
# callee's result Range from its fall-through value only; a `return`
# value is dropped. idx(k) may return 5, but the walk of get's body sees
# only the trailing 0 and proves list::at(xs, idx(k)) in range, so the
# program compiles with no handler and no errors clause -- then raises an
# undeclared IndexNotFound at run time (the default argv has one element,
# so k = 1). An exact argument (get(1)) is re-walked call-specifically and
# happens to be rejected; a value the proof cannot refine is not.
fn idx(k):
    if k > 0:
        return 5
    0

fn get(k):
    xs = [10, 20]
    list::at(xs, idx(k))

args = argv():
    on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
        []
get(list::length(args))
